    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: when a computed `customerCode` collides with an existing owner's `customerCode`, append `-<n>` with the smallest `n \u2265 2` that makes it unique, and return the de-duplicated code.\n\n**Change made** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`\n\nThe `AssignCustomerCode` OfficeFloor step previously assigned the raw computed code unconditionally. It now:\n\n1. Injects `OwnerRepository` (by type, per the OfficeFloor convention already used by `CheckUniqueIdentity`/`CountNamesakes`).\n2. Computes the base code via `OwnerIdentities.customerCode(owner)`.\n3. Collects existing owners' `customerCode` values (skipping self by id, as the step runs before `SaveOwner` so the new owner isn't persisted yet).\n4. If the base collides, appends `-2`, then `-3`, \u2026 choosing the smallest `n \u2265 2` not already taken, and stores the de-duplicated code.\n\nNo YAML change was needed \u2014 the step was already wired in `owners.POST.yml` before `SaveOwner`, and the repository resolves by type.\n\n**Tests:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u00b7 **BUILD SUCCESS** (the suite's `coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` acceptance test passes; a true hash collision can't be forced black-box, so the test asserts the rule's guarantee that distinct owners always get distinct codes).\n\nNote: two connectors (claude.ai Google Drive) reported needing authorization, but they weren't relevant to this task.",
